WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.230
Film Courage: How did you know the story [THE
VANISHING OF SIDNEY HALL] was special or maybe

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:04.230
you didn’t?

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.620
Shawn Christensen, Co-Writer/Director: I didn’t.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:14.809
We felt it was special because…I don’t
know because when you write a screenplay I

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:18.800
think you think it’s special.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:25.650
Whether or not it is special is kind of up
to the…you know…Lords of the Future.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:34.060
But we thought it was special…I guess because
we enjoyed writing with each other and we

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:39.380
enjoyed the story we had built and we had
some good plot twists and we liked the non-linear

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:48.350
structure and we had written…Jason [Dolan]
had written four, five or six screenplays

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:53.760
at that point and I had written two.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:01.750
And so when you get to your third script (your
third feature script), trust me you’ve learned

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:02.750
a lot.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:08.350
And I think us teaming up and his weaknesses
and strengths and my weaknesses and strengths

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.690
really worked well together.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:17.360
He’s very good at things that I’m not
and vice versa and so we liked it and we gave

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.890
it to ten of our friends.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:31.090
Here’s something that I would advise any
screenwriter to do who is starting out.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.370
You have a screenplay.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:39.100
Don’t send the PDF to a person and ask them
to read it.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.730
It’s just…you’re never going to get
anything with that.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:47.080
What you have to do is you have to make it
a special thing.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.720
What we did was we took ten friends, some
in the industry, some not (we didn’t know

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.200
many in the industry).

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.270
But friends that we trusted and thought they
were smart.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.140
And we said we have a hardcopy of a screenplay.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:05.979
We’d like you to come over to…(we had
an office that my co-writer was working for

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:09.670
someone with an office in Manhattan) and we
would like you to come over to the office

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:11.060
if you wouldn’t mind.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:18.120
We’re not going to stand over you like a
funny farm and watch you read the pages or

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.120
whatever.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.110
But we’d like you to read it and we’ll
walk away, we’ll be gone for a couple of

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:24.110
hours.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.110
You can write notes, whatever.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.580
You can bash it, we don’t care.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.860
We just want like ten different peoples points-of-view.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.800
So that’s what we did.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.250
We had ten people read it.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:38.350
Like they couldn’t leave with it, they couldn’t
take it, that way we know they’re going

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:40.880
to read it.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.630
And we’re not going to stand over their
shoulder and then we left the office and came

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.220
back and then we sit down with them and talk
it out with them.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:49.850
We took all of their notes.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:55.980
We decided what we were feeling and sort of
where the consensus notes were and then we

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:03.239
went right back to it and then two weeks later
we had a script that we felt perhaps could

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:08.360
be given to people of note.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:14.290
Film Courage: So you felt that in giving a
PDF that is would just sit inbox and they

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:18.120
would maybe find a reason to say “Oh, you
know what?

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.770
I’m so sorry, I’m really busy.”

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:24.820
But by having them there, sitting them down
with uninterrupted time, it was just a better

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.470
way to actually do it?

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.660
Shawn Christensen: You know, listen…that’s
a fact.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:31.790
Think about it in terms of being on the other
side of the coin.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:37.629
If your friend sends a PDF (a hundred-page
PDF) of something.

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:41.750
And maybe, by the way, you’re not a screenwriter,
you’re like their friend, you don’t really

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:43.530
read screenplays a lot.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.060
It’s a daunting task.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:49.500
It’s a task that…I don’t know how to
explain it.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.550
You could be excited about reading a screenplay
that comes into your inbox, but generally

00:03:53.550 --> 00:04:00.550
speaking in the age we live in with emails
and social media and all of these things for

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:05.160
people to get distracted by, to read your
friend or your cousin’s or your second cousin’s

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:11.111
or your friend’s brother’s uncle’s screenplay
or whatever, we felt that no one is going

00:04:11.111 --> 00:04:15.530
to read our script if we just send them a
PDF of it.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:22.999
Let’s just set a time for them to read it,
not be in the room when they read it but that

00:04:22.999 --> 00:04:29.360
way they’ll read it and then right after
they read it we get their fresh, unfiltered,

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.839
you know…like feelings about the screenplay.

00:04:33.839 --> 00:04:39.729
And then if we see things from like six out
of the ten of them are saying this is no good,

00:04:39.729 --> 00:04:42.530
well then we know (that this is something
we’ve got to work on).

